# Document-authoring data. This file is not imported by the robot.
F={}
def family(key,title,purpose,why,alternatives,library,refs):
    F[key]=dict(title=title,purpose=purpose,why=why,alternatives=alternatives,library=library,refs=refs)
family('integral','Integral images and local means','Obtain local sums and paper-background estimates without rescanning every window.','Rectangle inclusion and exclusion gives a fixed number of lookups per window after an image-sized prefix sum. It is inspectable and suits many large flat windows.','A direct window loop is simpler but repeats work. A separable sliding box filter has comparable linear image cost. A Gaussian weights the centre more and changes the estimated background.','SciPy ndimage.uniform_filter or OpenCV integral/boxFilter could replace parts. Match nearest-edge padding, window width and floating dtype; SciPy defaults to reflected borders. No accuracy gain follows from a matched arithmetic substitution; memory traffic and compiled loops determine speed.', 'crow1984,scipyuniform,opencvthreshold')
family('morph','Binary morphology','Repair narrow gaps or identify occupied neighbourhoods while controlling mask geometry.','Boolean dilation, erosion and closing express explicit geometric neighbourhood rules before seams or skeleton operations.','Opening removes small foreground features; closing fills gaps. A disk preserves a different distance notion from a square. Learned cleanup needs labelled examples and adds model dependencies.','SciPy binary_closing or OpenCV morphologyEx offers compiled morphology. Specify the same footprint and border treatment: this project erosion uses the number of in-image neighbours, so default outside-zero erosion need not agree. A different footprint changes segmentation, not just speed.','scipyclose,haralick1987')
family('threshold','Otsu and ink decisions','Separate handwriting from paper using a reproducible histogram criterion.','Otsu maximises between-class variance; dividing by estimated paper brightness first reduces multiplicative lighting changes. The ratio pipeline is a project construction around a published threshold rule.','Fixed grey thresholds are cheap but lighting-sensitive. Local mean or Sauvola thresholds adapt to local statistics but introduce their own windows and constants. None guarantees preservation of faint strokes.','OpenCV threshold with OTSU and skimage.filters.threshold_otsu implement the criterion, but quantisation, float ranges, ties and strict versus inclusive comparison must match. OpenCV adaptiveThreshold is a different method, not the same ratio calculation.','otsu1979,sauvola2000,opencvthreshold')
family('components','Connected components and run labelling','Identify whole ink pieces and measure their areas or bounds for ownership and artefact filtering.','Row runs reduce Python-level work on long spans; union operations connect labels across rows, including diagonal contacts.','Flood fill or pixel-wise union-find is easier to visualise but can visit many pixels in Python. Four-connectivity keeps diagonal contacts apart and therefore changes what counts as one object.','OpenCV connectedComponentsWithStats and SciPy ndimage.label provide compiled alternatives. Match eight-connectivity, background label and bounds conventions; numeric label IDs may differ even when the partitions agree. Faster labelling alone does not make ownership more correct.','he2008,tarjan1975,opencvshape,scipylabel')
family('resize','Sampling and geometric resampling','Fit captured lines to the trained canvas while limiting lost detail and retaining coordinate mappings.','Isotropic scaling preserves aspect ratio; separable filtering and normalised edge weights make the resampler explicit. Widened shrink filters reduce aliasing.','Nearest-neighbour suits labels but aliases grey strokes. Bilinear sampling is simple; bicubic or Lanczos changes ringing, sharpness and support. Independent axis stretching distorts writer shape.','Pillow resize and OpenCV resize/warpAffine are available. Their kernels, centre coordinates, antialiasing and rounding need not match this implementation. They can reduce Python overhead; recognition accuracy must be evaluated after any changed pixels.','wolberg1990,pillowimage,opencvtransform')
family('filter','Blur and elastic displacement','Smooth signals or create controlled training perturbations without changing labels deliberately.','Separable spatial filters and smooth displacement fields provide controllable local variation. Their strengths are settings, not naturally correct noise models.','Median filtering rejects impulse noise but changes stroke geometry differently. Bilateral smoothing uses intensity similarity. Unrestricted deformation can change the character and violate its label.','SciPy gaussian_filter or OpenCV GaussianBlur/remap can implement related operations. Kernel truncation, boundary mode and dtype affect results; compiled filtering may be faster, but no unconditional timing or accuracy claim is established here.','simard2003,scipygaussian,opencvtransform')
family('pitch','Line pitch and row profiles','Locate line centres and choose scale-aware segmentation distances before character recognition.','Projection peaks, a letter-height floor, median gaps and autocorrelation supply interpretable estimates when line layouts vary.','Straight minima work for clean separate rows but cut overlapping strokes. Hough line estimates favour straighter structures. A learned line detector needs annotated regions and another model.','SciPy find_peaks/correlate can supply primitives, not the complete floor/fallback/merging policy. Prominence and distance settings must be matched. Better peak detection is not automatically better ink ownership.','postl1986,scipypeaks')
family('deskew','Orientation and skew estimation','Align the page line axis while preserving the writer slant needed for reproduction.','The row-profile concentration objective is inexpensive and distinct from deslanting. Quarter turns resolve axis; reader-based inversion resolves which end is up.','Hough estimates and connected-component baselines can estimate skew; perspective rectification handles projective distortion that one rotation cannot. No formal evidence makes the selected angle grid universal.','OpenCV warpAffine or Pillow rotate can apply a chosen angle, but neither substitutes for the project score and search. Interpolation and expansion affect the classifier input.','postl1986,wolberg1990,opencvtransform,pillowops')
family('seam','Minimum cost seams','Find curved boundaries between lines without relying on a completely blank horizontal row.','Dynamic programming minimises accumulated ink, location and vertical-step costs inside a corridor, with saved predecessors recovering the actual path.','Straight cuts are cheaper but cannot bend around ascenders. Graph cuts or shortest-path packages offer more general models with added setup and constraints. Learned segmentation needs labelled boundary examples.','A SciPy shortest-path representation could encode the grid, but materialising a graph is unnecessary overhead for this acyclic recurrence. Compiling the same recurrence may reduce loop cost; it does not validate its ink weights.','bellman1954,arvanitopoulos2014,avidan2007')
family('ownership','Ink ownership and crop construction','Keep dots, rings and tails with the intended line instead of blindly following a seam boundary.','Project-specific component, overlap and size guards target handwriting ambiguity that a purely spatial partition cannot settle.','Whole-component majority voting is simpler but can move too much of a touching line. Nearest-centre assignment ignores stem support. A learned owner classifier needs pixel/component annotations.','Connected-component and region-property libraries can accelerate measurements; there is no one-call equivalent for these exact correction rules. Their thresholds require project evidence, not an algorithm citation.','code2026,opencvshape')
family('conv','Convolution and feature sequences','Extract local stroke features shared across locations before sequence recognition or writer pooling.','Weight sharing reduces parameters compared with a fully connected image mapping. Patch-to-matrix multiplication reaches compiled NumPy linear algebra while exposing the computation.','Fully connected images ignore locality; larger kernels have more parameters; residual backbones add skip paths and different capacity. A replacement normally requires new weights.','PyTorch Conv2d offers CPU/GPU backend kernels and autodiff. Match cross-correlation, weights, stride, padding and dtype for arithmetic comparisons. The current NumPy layer stacks one matrix product per batch item; stride views can still lead to reshape copies. No fixed speed factor is asserted.','shi2017,torchconv,numpystride,numpymatmul')
family('bn','Batch normalisation','Control activation scale during learning and apply stored training statistics during inference.','Learned scale and offset preserve representational freedom after normalisation; inference folds fixed statistics into an affine calculation.','Layer normalisation normalises a different set of values and does not use the same batch statistics. Omitting normalisation changes learning dynamics and usually requires retraining.','PyTorch BatchNorm2d distinguishes train/eval state and running statistics. Match epsilon, momentum convention and biased/unbiased variance treatment. A numerical match does not establish equal independently trained accuracy.','ioffe2015,torchbn')
family('activation','Nonlinear activation and affine transforms','Let stacked learned layers express nonlinear image and sequence relationships, or convert features into class scores.','ReLU passes positive responses; sigmoid gates bound retention factors; tanh bounds a candidate state. An affine layer combines features with learned biases.','Leaky ReLU avoids a zero negative slope; GELU is smoother but costs more and changes the network. A gate needs a bounded function rather than ordinary ReLU. These are architecture decisions, not interchangeable inference switches.','PyTorch supplies Linear, ReLU, Sigmoid and related layers. NumPy already executes elementwise primitives in compiled loops. Small standalone library calls may cost more overhead than they save; fused network execution has different trade-offs.','glorotrect,gravesbook,torchlinear')
family('pool','Pooling and sequence formation','Reduce spatial resolution or summarise a feature map into sequence columns and writer features.','Two spatial pools reduce work; height maximum produces one feature vector per horizontal position; writer width averaging summarises the line.','Average instead of maximum retains average activation; strided convolution learns reduction; attention pooling adds trained weights. Every alternative changes the function and may require retraining.','PyTorch pooling layers are equivalents only under the same axes, windows and edge rules. This project local max implementation is designed for the invoked two-by-two, stride-two case; its general signature is not proof of every library-supported configuration.','shi2017,torchpool')
family('softmax','Softmax and log sum exp','Turn logits into a normalised class distribution and sum small probabilities stably.','Subtracting the maximum leaves ratios unchanged while preventing exponential overflow. Log-domain path sums avoid underflow in CTC.','Direct exponentiation is simpler but can overflow. Sigmoid treats classes independently and does not express the same exclusive-class distribution. Temperature changes confidence without necessarily changing argmax.','SciPy special.logsumexp and PyTorch log_softmax provide stable primitives and autodiff. Equal formulae can differ in rounding/reduction order. Calibrated real-world confidence does not follow from normalisation alone.','gravesbook,scipylogsum,torchnumeric')
family('lstm','LSTM and bidirectional recurrence','Use left and right image context to disambiguate sequential character features.','Additive cell memory and multiplicative gates offer a controlled route for retaining information; reverse recurrence is available because the complete offline line is known.','A plain RNN has simpler state but difficult long dependencies. GRU uses fewer gates. Attention/transformers offer another context model and different cost, data and checkpoint requirements.','PyTorch LSTM provides optimised recurrent kernels and autodiff. Gate order, two bias arrays, direction reversal and tensor layout must match. Fused kernels can avoid Python frame loops; speed and final trained accuracy are separate comparisons.','hochreiter1997,torchlstm,gravesbook')
family('ctc','CTC decoding and forced alignment','Train from complete line transcripts and recover transcript-constrained character locations for extraction.','CTC marginalises valid blank/repeat paths; greedy decoding selects a path cheaply; forced alignment maximises over paths for a supplied known transcript.','Manual character boxes need annotation and unreliable cursive cuts. Beam search can improve transcript search at extra cost. Attention decoding learns a different alignment mechanism.','PyTorch CTCLoss computes the training loss, not the entire greedy/forced-alignment pipeline. Its mean reduction divides by target length, unlike the project batch mean. Align lengths, blank, precision and reduction before numerical comparison.','graves2006,ctcdistill,torchctc')
family('skeleton','Skeletonisation and centreline recovery','Convert thick ink areas into approximate pen paths and normalise stroke width.','Zhang--Suen deletion rules preserve local connectivity while thinning in two simultaneous sub-passes. This fits the geometric path representation.','Medial-axis transforms use distance information and may branch differently. Contours trace edges rather than one pen centreline. Neither recovers actual stroke order or pressure from a photograph.','skimage.morphology.skeletonize(method=zhang) and OpenCV contrib thinning are related implementations. Match binary polarity, padding and iteration rules. Lookup tables/compiled loops may reduce repeated-array cost; the resulting path is still an estimate.','zhangsuen1984,skmorph')
family('trace','Skeleton graph tracing and continuation','Turn centreline pixels into ordered polylines, then prune stubs and reconnect plausible continuations.','Endpoints, degrees and edge visitation provide explicit topology. Angle tests use opposing endpoint directions to distinguish continuation from a sharp turn.','Contour tracing follows an outline, not this graph. Euler or route optimisation changes traversal, not the original unknown pen order. A learned tracer adds training and ambiguity.','Graph packages could manage connectivity but per-pixel object graphs add overhead. Image libraries can supply skeletons/contours, not guaranteed original pen chronology. The project uses diagonal suppression and project thresholds.','zhangsuen1984,code2026')
family('simplify','Douglas Peucker simplification','Reduce path vertices and command count while retaining meaningful geometric detail.','Recursive farthest-deviation tests keep corners that exceed a tolerance. The current routine uses distance to the supporting line and a near-loop split.','Uniform vertex skipping ignores shape. Distance-based decimation is cheaper but does not provide the same deviation test. Spline fitting changes representation and requires sampling/tolerance choices.','OpenCV approxPolyDP is a related Douglas--Peucker implementation. Endpoint handling, closed paths and distance convention can differ. Compiled execution may reduce recursion overhead; equal epsilon does not by itself guarantee identical vertices.','douglaspeucker1973,opencvshape')
family('glyph','Alignment guided glyph extraction','Recover labelled character geometry from offline lines without manually supplied character boxes.','The known transcript constrains CTC; shared ordered cuts, body-band estimates and shear normalisation separate shape from source size and spacing.','Connected-component letters work mainly for separated print. Independent cuts can collapse into the same gap. Direct generative synthesis needs extra training and does not expose these stored shapes.','A library OCR aligner can supply another alignment distribution, but changing it changes extraction quality and provenance. NumPy, OpenCV or SciPy supply parts; the complete cut/ownership/profile policy is project-specific.','graves2006,vinciarelli2001,code2026')
family('descriptor','Occupancy descriptors and shape distances','Reject malformed cuts and compare stored shapes without requiring matching vertex counts.','A sampled twelve-by-twelve occupancy grid, square-root compression, blur and unit norm provide a compact shift-tolerant representation. Width gates separately retain aspect information lost by box normalisation.','Raw pixel differences are sensitive to translation; point-set distances require registration; learned embeddings need training. Higher grid resolution keeps detail but is less tolerant of shifts.','SciPy distances and image resizers offer primitives; a learned embedding is not an arithmetic replacement. Faster distance computation preserves the metric only if normalisation and sampling are identical.','code2026,bulacu2007')
family('medoid','Consensus medoids and prototypes','Choose representative writer glyphs while rejecting fragmented outliers.','A medoid minimises summed within-pool L1 descriptor distance and remains an actual sample. A denoised prototype combines compatible resampled strokes; it is a separate constructed object.','A mean descriptor can be unrepresentative and supplies no pen path directly. Clustering handles multiple modes but needs a cluster rule. One arbitrarily chosen sample has no consensus guarantee.','Pairwise-distance libraries can compute the same matrix; generic clustering is a different selection policy. Small pools limit the possible speed benefit. No library can fix a pool whose samples all contain bad cuts.','code2026,haines2016')
family('synth','Glyph placement and candidate search','Generate new text in a stored writer style and balance readability with model-based writer identity.','Explicit glyphs suit a small offline collection and expose size, slant, spacing, joins and fallback provenance. Repeated candidates and accepted repair search this finite representation.','Recurrent stroke generators need online trajectories; GANwriting and One-DM produce raster images and add generative training plus path recovery. They may model context better but are not drop-in arithmetic replacements.','PyTorch can implement neural generators or accelerate judging networks; it does not supply this project glyph library and joining policy as one operation. No measured synthesis quality or time advantage is claimed for an untested replacement.','haines2016,gravesgenerate,ganwriting,onedm')
family('render','Trajectory rasterisation','Make an image of a generated path for preview and recognition-based scoring.','Sampling straight segments and applying a controlled width makes candidate rendering explicit. The uniform width models one drawing pen rather than source ink intensity.','Vector display keeps exact paths for viewing but classifiers need rasters. Antialiased line drawing and supersampling change edge pixels and cost. Rendering width is not physical pressure.','Pillow ImageDraw or OpenCV polylines can rasterise paths. Coverage rules, antialiasing, round caps and blur alter classifier inputs. Pixel similarity and performance should be compared separately.','pillowdraw,opencvdraw,code2026')
family('motion','Coordinate mapping and path planning','Place and fit the generated trajectory into the measured machine work area.','Uniform scaling preserves shape while margins/origin define placement. Row grouping and nearest-end stroke order reduce travel; clamping makes out-of-bounds points explicit.','Anisotropic fitting distorts glyphs. Optimal travelling-salesperson routing is more expensive and still must respect stroke/row semantics. A fixed sequence preserves order but may travel farther.','Geometry libraries provide affine transforms and route solvers, but hardware bounds and pen semantics remain application-specific. Algorithmic path length is different from measured motion time.','bresenham1965,code2026')
family('bresenham','Bresenham stepping and feed timing','Coordinate two integer step counts along one line using a bounded error accumulator.','Major-axis stepping distributes minor-axis pulses without a division per step. Feed conversion turns desired length and rate into a nominal pulse delay.','Independent axis moves draw an L shape. DDA uses fractional accumulation. Dedicated motion firmware can add acceleration and more deterministic pulse timing.','An image-library line drawer generates pixels, not GPIO pulses. A motion controller or kernel/hardware pulse engine is the relevant timing alternative. Geometry equivalence cannot establish equal scheduling, missed steps or physical accuracy.','bresenham1965,a4988datasheet,kernelgpio')
family('pen','Pen state and switch confirmation','Avoid unnecessary one-way cam toggles and wait for the requested feedback state.','An idempotent state request does nothing when already at target; finite confirmation, timeout and motor-off paths bound actuation. Switch state does not measure contact pressure.','A servo gives positional commands; a third stepper adds another axis; hardware debounce filters electrical transitions. These are hardware/design alternatives rather than interchangeable constants.','GPIO libraries handle line access; they do not define cam geometry, debounce policy or pressure feedback. Hardware timing can be steadier than userspace sleeps. No new reliability or force measurement is inferred.','kernelgpio,rohmnpn,code2026')
family('calibrate','Homing and axis calibration','Establish a reference and convert commanded pulse counts into machine coordinates.','Counting pulses between switches against measured travel estimates steps/mm; clearance retracts keep drawing away from the contacts.','Nominal belt geometry avoids a measurement but misses actual scale errors. Encoders observe motion continuously at extra hardware cost. Multi-point calibration can assess squareness and backlash beyond one scale factor.','Motion-control firmware can home and scale axes but still needs correct wiring, travel and configuration. A library cannot supply missing reference measurements.','a4988datasheet,bresenham1965,code2026')
family('gcode','G code parsing and job generation','Connect abstract pen paths to modal movement and pen-state commands.','Explicit units, absolute positioning, feeds and dwell make a job inspectable; calibrated execution maintains a commanded position and checks interruptions.','Direct streaming avoids a stored file but loses a convenient review/replay artefact. Standard firmware has a richer parser and motion planner, requiring agreed dialect and pen-code mapping.','A G-code parser can recognise syntax but cannot assume this project M3/M5 pen semantics or P dwell units. LinuxCNC/GRBL-style conventions must be checked rather than treated as identical.','code2026,linuxcnc')
family('metrics','Edit distance and evaluation metrics','Separate text recovery, writer decisions, geometric agreement and physical accuracy.','Each metric answers a different requirement. Pooled character edits weight characters; writer decisions are binary per line; geometric F measures thresholded point proximity.','Exact-line match is stricter but less diagnostic. WER edits differ from word LCS recall. IoU, Pearson correlation and human judgements are distinct quantities and cannot be relabelled interchangeably.','Edit-distance libraries can accelerate dynamic programming; SciPy spatial trees or distance transforms can compute nearest-point distances. Match normalisation, empty cases, tolerance and aggregation. Faster scoring does not improve handwriting.','levenshtein1966,tanksmetric,nistinterval')
family('optim','Optimisers schedules and gradient clipping','Turn analytic gradients into stable parameter updates and manage training step sizes.','RMSprop adapts using recent squared gradients; AdamW separates weight shrinkage from adaptive gradient scaling; clipping limits gradient norm. These are training mechanisms, not live inference controls.','SGD momentum is simpler but has different scaling. Coupled L2 and decoupled AdamW are not identical. Elementwise clipping changes direction unlike global norm clipping.','PyTorch optimisers automate state and updates. Defaults, epsilon placement, moment bias correction and scheduler counters must match. Faster training is not proof of better final validation accuracy.','kingma2015,loshchilov2019,pascanu2013,rmslecture')
family('backward','Analytic differentiation and training caches','Compute parameter derivatives without relying on framework automatic differentiation.','Saved forward intermediates let the chain rule propagate one loss derivative through convolution, normalisation, recurrence and pooling. Overlapping input patches must add their contributions.','Finite differences independently check selected coordinates but cost two loss evaluations per coordinate and suffer cancellation. Automatic differentiation reduces manual algebra but still needs correct forward semantics.','PyTorch autograd supplies reverse differentiation and can be a numerical reference. Match dtype, reduction, state and nonsmooth-point convention. A close gradient comparison validates checked operations, not every dataset or trained model.','gravesbook,ioffe2015,torchnumeric')
family('init','Initialisation and learned parameter shapes','Start training with a controlled signal scale and checkpoint-compatible array layout.','Fan-in scaled convolution and explicit tensor shapes expose variance assumptions. Current linear and LSTM initialisers use uniform bounds; they are not the old Gaussian/Xavier descriptions.','Zero weights preserve unwanted symmetry. Xavier and He gains use different activation assumptions. Orthogonal recurrent matrices are another training choice. None yields a useful reader before learning.','PyTorch initialisers provide equivalent distributions if bounds, dtype, bias and random generator are matched. The same seed across different libraries need not draw the same arrays.','he2015,glorot2010,torchlstm,torchlinear')
family('augment','Training data augmentation','Expose the fixed-label learner to plausible capture and writing variation.','Small affine, perspective and elastic changes broaden the training distribution without collecting a new label for each transformed image. Validation remains deterministic.','Collecting real varied captures avoids a synthetic-noise assumption but costs data effort. Large distortions can change a character or writer cue. Test-time augmentation is a different inference procedure.','Torchvision/OpenCV/Pillow transforms can implement similar warps. Interpolation, coordinate convention and random distributions differ; stronger or faster augmentation is not automatically more accurate.','simard2003,wolberg1990,opencvtransform')
family('dataset','Datasets partitions and sampling','Keep image and label identities aligned and separate held-out evaluation from learning.','Seeded splits and personal/public sampling expose exactly which population drives learning; caches avoid repeated decoding. Labels are not used as classifier answers during ordinary inference.','Page-disjoint partitions reduce shared capture leakage; writer-disjoint testing asks a different question from closed-set writer naming. Oversampling changes representation, not the number of independent examples.','Framework Dataset/DataLoader objects organise batches and workers, not the validity of a partition. More worker processes can duplicate cache/model memory. Actual split manifests and run metadata outrank defaults.','marti2002,code2026,torchdata')
family('codec','Image codecs and cached crop bytes','Convert stored image bytes into arrays and retain compact intermediate examples.','PNG reconstruction uses prediction filters and lossless decompression; JPEG uses entropy decoding and inverse DCT. This exposes training-file arithmetic, while the demo server uses Pillow for image ingestion.','Pillow/OpenCV decoders support many formats and compiled paths. Writing a decoder trades coverage and maintenance effort for explicit understanding. JPEG is lossy; PNG is lossless relative to encoded pixels.','Pillow/libjpeg or OpenCV imread is the practical library alternative. Exact JPEG rounding and colour conversion can differ. Do not claim file-byte equality or recognition equivalence from a decoder name alone.','pngspec,jpegspec,pillowimage')
family('checkpoint','Checkpoint and log persistence','Preserve weights, class mappings and evidence about a training run.','Named arrays and metadata make inference state inspectable and support interrupted work. A best checkpoint is chosen by a stated criterion rather than final epoch alone.','Pickled framework checkpoints carry richer objects but add trust/version concerns. Saving only weights is simpler but cannot reproduce optimiser continuation by itself.','NumPy savez and framework state_dict formats are storage mechanisms, not evidence of the host, exact run or accuracy. Reloading files does not automatically replace already-instantiated server objects.','code2026,numpysavez')
family('server','Web orchestration and job state','Connect capture, inference, profile selection, files and calibrated drawing through explicit requests.','Separate stages and saved jobs make the flow reviewable. Shared models/profiles avoid reloading on every request; current imports determine the real API contract.','A monolithic request is simpler but couples generation and hardware execution. A queue/worker service scales differently and needs explicit state/failure handling.','Flask handles routing and responses, Pillow handles images, and NumPy performs arithmetic. These are actual dependencies; the complete demo cannot truthfully be called library-free. File sanitisation/API keys do not constitute full external-service security.','flaskdocs,pillowops,code2026')
family('workers','Independent line workers','Run independent text or writer passes on multiple processes while restoring page order.','Explicit fork workers inherit configured models and cap numerical threads to reduce oversubscription. The serial path remains available; startup and IPC still cost time.','Serial processing avoids pool overhead for short pages. Threads share memory but interact with Python and BLAS scheduling. Spawn workers need explicit model initialisation rather than this fork-inherited configuration.','The code already uses Python multiprocessing.Pool and NumPy/BLAS. Fork is copy-on-write, not a guarantee of no allocation; passing crops/results still needs communication. Forking a threaded server with native numerical state is a deployment consideration. No new speedup or identical-output test was run.','pythonmp,numpymatmul')
family('camera','Camera capture and image orientation','Acquire a frame and put its pixel orientation in agreement with the browser and segmenter.','An existing preview handle avoids competing camera opens; warm-up frames allow acquisition to settle. EXIF transpose applies metadata to pixels rather than assuming the viewer appearance equals array orientation.','Direct V4L2 access offers lower-level controls; a CSI camera uses a different acquisition stack. Reading one immediate frame may capture an unsettled exposure/focus state.','OpenCV VideoCapture and Pillow ImageOps.exif_transpose are already used. Their presence concerns acquisition/decoding and orientation, not a claim that OpenCV implements the custom segmentation. Sensor claims need actual negotiated settings.','opencvcamera,pillowops')
family('timing','Timing and requirement accounting','Measure request wall time and retain earlier generation work when drawing is requested later.','perf_counter differences measure elapsed time; Carry adds external work while Add only labels work already inside the wall interval. This avoids double counting stages.','CPU time excludes waiting; summing nested stages can double-count; theoretical path time ignores overhead. A benchmark on another host is not an embedded qualification.','Python perf_counter is already the timing primitive. Profilers can attribute cost more finely but add overhead. Multiprocessing stage wall time is not the sum of worker CPU times.','pythontime,code2026')
family('curate','Profile curation','Remove selected bad glyphs and rebuild representatives without training a new classifier.','Explicit variant indices and a saved curated profile make manual quality selection inspectable. A classifier label remains the base writer, not a new class for the suffix.','Automatic prior/consensus filtering scales better but can retain systematic bad cuts. Human selection adds judgement and should not be hidden when interpreting results.','Pillow draws inspection sheets; geometry and pool policy remain project-specific. A library UI or clustering tool changes workflow rather than proving better style.','code2026,haines2016,pillowdraw')
family('retrace','Direct ink retracing','Bypass recognition and glyph synthesis to compare a traced source path with physical output.','It removes two sources of variation but still includes binarisation, thinning, tracing, pruning, fitting and motion; it is not a pure mechanical ground truth.','Direct known geometric paths isolate motion more tightly. OCR-and-synthesise asks a different end-to-end question. An online digitiser would supply a better observed trajectory.','Image/vector tracing libraries could replace centreline extraction, with different topology and simplification. The current PageStrokes timer keyword is incompatible with current SegmentLines and is documented as a static integration discrepancy.','code2026,zhangsuen1984,douglaspeucker1973')

